      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i checked the temp and it seems to be steady at around 390f/200c, and the pressure is definitely high enough. the ink is getting on the paper fine but when we lift the sheet the pigment just isn't sticking to the fibers like it should. it's like the color is just... missing or not making the jump. could it be a humidity issue in the factory or something with the ink sublimation time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The temperature should indeed be around the range required for polyester sublimation, but 390F is slightly too high, as it can lead to a \"blown\" or etched print effect due to excessive heat. Try reducing the temperature to fall within the recommended 288-324F range. Concerning ink sublimation time, ensure it's sufficient; typically, colors need about 35 seconds to fully transfer, though this may vary if you have multiple colors. A humidity issue in the factory can cause many problems, including affecting ink adhesion or transferring - ensure you're within the ideal range of 40-60% humidity (depending on your specific paper and ink). Adjustments here may prevent the ink from properly fusing with the fabric fibers.",
