      "content": "Absolutely, high humidity can severely impact the sublimation process. When humidity levels are too high, it not only affects the sublimation but can also cause issues with drying. The moisture in the air can interfere with the ink's ability to properly adhere to the fabric fibers, resulting in low-quality print, particularly in vibrant and saturated colors that are more sensitive to these environmental conditions. Aim to keep the factory air at a consistent 40-60% humidity to circumvent this issue. If your facility's humidity is too high, consider using a dehumidifier or adjusting the ventilation to ensure a more controlled environment. This should help the polymerized inks transition from the paper to the polyester in full color density.",
